module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_precondition_check_sits_downstream_of_the_irreversible_effect

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data a_precondition_check_sits_downstream_of_the_irreversible_effect: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_precondition_check_sits_downstream_of_the_irreversible_effect" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "an effect-ordering analysis over a func's reachable effect sequence that refuses when a readonly effect, or an effect whose required permissions are not established by that point, is reachable AFTER a non-idempotent mutation. COMPLETE CLIMB CONDITION, stated once so this field and the receipts cannot disagree about what lifts the class: (a) the analysis, which is sufficient for the mint-then-store population this row was filed against, where the step that would have refused is an effect carrying required permissions (gcp.SecretManager.CreateSecret behind cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create); AND (b) discharge of the carrier-fixture specimen's tracked repair, gunb-ai/gunbc#16656, which is PENDING UNTIL EXECUTED EVIDENCE. The fixture is now a member of this class's population and its later step is a pure path predicate rather than an effect with required permissions, so the analysis ALONE leaves that admitted instance exposed -- and neither half alone lifts the class." } },

  receipts: [
    "**a check that would have refused, placed after the effect it was protecting, is not a weak wall -- it is a wall built on the wrong side of the door, and the ordering claim in the prose beside it is what stops anyone looking** (INVALID STATE: a flow declares that every precondition is decided before its irreversible operation, and one of those preconditions is in fact an effect performed AFTER it. Each step is individually correct and the sequence type-checks; only the ORDER is wrong. HARM: the protected operation is non-idempotent and its product is returned exactly once, so the refusal arrives after the only copy of the value has been created, and the failure mode is not a failed run but an orphaned live credential -- a thing that exists, grants access, and which nothing in the tree knows the identity of.",

    "RECEIPT, MEASURED ON THIS REPOSITORY: gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint_run carries the annotation 'EVERY PRECONDITION IS DECIDED BEFORE THE CREATE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION', and enumerates the ones it decides -- an allocated account id, a pinned bootstrap custody version, an admitted mint shape. The CUSTODY CONTAINER was not among them. store_minted_token called gcp.SecretManager.CreateSecret AFTER cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create had already minted the token, so a run lacking permission to create the container would mint a live R2 credential and then fail to store it. The mint's own refusal text has always said the right thing -- 'THE TOKEN WAS MINTED BUT ITS CUSTODY CONTAINER WAS REFUSED, so the value is unrecoverable and the token must be revoked' -- which is the tell: the flow had ALREADY NAMED this outcome as a disaster and had placed the check that prevents it downstream anyway.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVED ITS ONE EXECUTION, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A CLASS. gunbc#10947 ran this flow to completion and stored the read mint. It succeeded because the identity executing it was an operator gcloud session holding project-wide secretmanager.secrets.create, so CreateSecret returned 200 and the misordering was never observable. A GREEN EXECUTION UNDER THE MOST PRIVILEGED AVAILABLE IDENTITY IS THE WEAKEST EVIDENCE AN ORDERING CLAIM CAN HAVE: privilege makes every downstream permission check succeed, which is exactly the condition under which a check's POSITION stops mattering. The defect became visible only when the flow was pointed at a least-privilege federated identity -- not by anyone re-reading the code.",

    "DUAL OF THE FABRICATION-DOWNSTREAM-OF-THE-CHECK CLASS, AND NAMED SEPARATELY BECAUSE THE REMEDIES ARE OPPOSITE. gunbc#10739 recorded a fabricated value placed downstream of the check that catches the real value's absence: the check is correctly positioned and something false flows past it. Here the check is correctly SHAPED and incorrectly POSITIONED: nothing is fabricated, the refusal is honest, and it arrives too late to be worth anything. One is repaired by deleting the fabrication, the other by moving the check -- and a reader who has only the first row in mind will read this code as conforming, because nothing in it is fabricating anything.",

    "THE TEMPTING REPAIR IS ITSELF AN INSTANCE, AND IT IS WHAT THE AUTHOR PROPOSED FIRST. Faced with 'the identity cannot create the container', the cheap fix is to have an operator pre-create it and teach the actuator to read a 409 ALREADY_EXISTS as converged. That leaves the irreversible effect ahead of the check and merely stops one of its consequences from being reported as an error (operator ruling, 2026-09-12: 'mapping a 409 after the Create to container-present would leave the irreversible effect ahead of the check, which is the bug you found, not a fix for it'). The real repair is that the flow stops performing the write at all: the container becomes an OBSERVED precondition -- a readonly GET decided inside the same ladder as every other precondition -- and the write path narrows to the one operation that was always meant to follow the mint.",

    "NOT THE SAME ROW AS [[enforcement_gate_positioned_after_the_effect_it_guards]], AND THE TEST THAT SEPARATES THEM IS THAT ROW'S OWN NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER. Checked deliberately, because the two read as twins and section 3 forbids a twin. There, a refusal is COMPUTED BEFORE the effect and the path fails to BRANCH on it -- the verdict is in hand, correct, and only ever read at serialization time, so the run changes the world and then reports accurately that it should not have. Here NO VERDICT EXISTS at the moment of the irreversible effect, because the operation that would produce it is sequenced after: every branch is present and every branch is correct at its own site, and gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint_run did refuse honestly when CreateSecret failed -- it simply refused about a token that had already been minted. The remedies are correspondingly opposite: branch on a value you already have, versus hoist an observation you never took and delete a write.",

    "THAT DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TAXONOMIC, WHICH IS WHY THE ROWS STAY SEPARATE. The landed row's trigger is an effect-ordering reader that refuses any path reaching a declared irreversible operation WITHOUT FIRST BRANCHING ON THE GUARD NAMING IT. Run that mechanism against this defect and it passes clean: no guard named cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create, and the CreateSecret refusal that existed named CreateSecret. A capability that fully closes that class leaves this one untouched, so merging the rows would retire this one behind a wall that cannot see it. The axis those rows already share is stated in the landed row's own boundary against [[declared_refusal_unreachable_from_its_own_constructor]] -- there the arm is never reached, there it is reached too late -- and this is the third point on it: the arm is reached, on time relative to its own operation, and that operation is itself the thing sequenced too late.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: FOR EVERY NON-IDEMPOTENT OR VALUE-RETURNED-ONCE OPERATION, LIST EVERY EFFECT THAT RUNS AFTER IT AND ASK OF EACH WHETHER IT COULD HAVE RUN BEFORE. Any effect that could have is a precondition sitting in the wrong place, whatever its position in the source suggests. The sharpest form of the question is about PERMISSIONS rather than logic: an operation appearing after the irreversible one needs an authorization nobody has checked for by then, so the ordering defect and the least-privilege defect are the same defect, and the flow that runs under an over-privileged identity can hold both indefinitely without either becoming visible.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the mitigation was a human reading the actuator's call order while deriving the IAM cells a federated identity would need -- not any executing check. CEILING: structurally guaranteed. The facts needed to decide this are all modeled -- which operations mutate (extdeps.transports.rest carries RestReadExchange against RestMutationExchange, and the service declarations carry `readonly`), and which permission each effect requires (extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_manager secret_manager_effect_required_permissions) -- so a flow that performs a mutation whose reachable successors include an effect requiring an unheld or unchecked authorization is derivable rather than reviewable. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a CAPABILITY: an effect-ordering analysis over a func's reachable effect sequence that refuses when a readonly effect, or an effect whose required permissions are not established by that point, is reachable AFTER a non-idempotent mutation. Moving this one check, or adding one arm to this one ladder, satisfies none of it and leaves every other mint-then-store flow in the tree exposed identically.",

    "STANDING, READ FROM THIS ROW'S OWN EVIDENCE -- this row carried rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated }, which its own prose had already outgrown: an unread reading asserts that the blocker class cannot be told from the receipts, and these receipts state a ceiling, give its reason, and name a capability as the next trigger, so nothing about the blocker was in fact unstated. FOUND AT: mitigatable, per the RUNG FOUND AT receipt -- the only thing that has ever caught this ordering was a human reading the actuator's call order while deriving the IAM cells a federated identity would need, not any executing check, which is the definition of a mitigation rather than a guarantee. CURRENT RUNG: mitigatable, unchanged as filed; there is no CURRENT RUNG, RUNG NOW or CLIMBED receipt in this row, so by the ledger's own rule (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode: where no receipt states a later rung, the standing is the rung as filed) the current rung is the rung as filed and not a higher one somebody expects. CEILING: structurally guaranteed, for the reason the CEILING receipt gives -- which operations mutate and which permission each effect requires are already modeled, so the property is derivable rather than reviewable. BLOCKER CLASS: climbable but unbuilt; the trigger names an analysis no admitted code performs, and nothing about it waits on a declaration that does not yet exist, so it is not AwaitsOneGrounding. THE TYPED TRIGGER is the effect-ordering analysis AND discharge of the carrier-fixture specimen's tracked repair at gunb-ai/gunbc#16656, which is PENDING UNTIL EXECUTED EVIDENCE: one field, one complete climb condition, so this receipt cannot claim a climb that the typed field withholds. The analysis alone is NOT sufficient -- the fixture is a member of this class's population, and a class with an admitted instance left exposed has not been climbed. Nor is the specimen's repair a rung climb by itself: it is a local repair of one flow (SPECIMEN REPAIR below), named here by tracked identity rather than by a conversational label, so the referent outlives this thread and cannot be read as already done. The class stays at its ceiling gap until BOTH land.",

    "SPECIMEN, located 2026-11-17 by dashboard review 96213 on PR #16646 -- in a test fixture rather than in production, which is what makes it cheap to state precisely: test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier_fixture::roadmap_event_carrier_mint_scratch hands the CALLER'S template to shell.Mktemp.DirWithTemplate and applies its path admission only to whatever path comes back. Its three refusal arms are exemplary -- each typed, located, loud -- and they DISAGREE WITH EACH OTHER about whether a refusal cleans up: the shape arm returns without reclaiming what it just created, while the seal arm removes the directory it made before refusing. One function, one refusal posture, opposite residue. That disagreement is the tell, and it is invisible to the review that catches everything else here, because a reader auditing error handling sees three correct refusals and moves on; the defect is the position of the check relative to the create, and no fold over failure arms can see it, since each arm is correct at the moment it is chosen. The harm is reachable even though every checked-in caller is careful: the mint is exported and the template is unrestricted, so an artifact is created at a path the refusing code had already decided it must not touch -- measured on the runtime box, /var/tmp is mode 1777 drwxrwxrwt, so an unprivileged uid 1000 creates there successfully and is refused only afterwards -- what the refusal does not carry is a certificate of RECLAMATION. It says a great deal about creation, and my own control guidance depends on that: in test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier_fixture, type RoadmapEventCarrierMint's RoadmapEventCarrierMintRefused { detail: String } variant distinguishes 'mktemp could not mint a scratch from template ' (nothing was ever created) from 'the directory minted at ' (something was created and is being refused) from 'the fixture could not plant its ownership seal inside ' -- so the refusal identifies that a directory was minted, but it does not certify that the mint was reclaimed. The shape-refusal branch returns without cleanup, leaving the directory behind; only the seal branch appends any account of removal, via roadmap_carrier_mint_cleanup_words, which is the wording the other two branches never reach. A caller that reads the presence of a loud diagnostic as implying a tidy-up is the failure mode this row exists to prevent.",

    "SPECIMEN REPAIR, LOCAL TO THIS INSTANCE AND NOT A CLASS TRIGGER, added with the carrier-fixture specimen. This instance's repair is a local one and it is not a rung climb of the class: the tracked repair gunb-ai/gunbc#16656 -- admitting the template before creation and verifying provenance after creation with reclaim on refusal -- closes THIS specimen by construction, and that PR is PENDING UNTIL EXECUTED EVIDENCE: until it carries such evidence the specimen stays open and the class stays at its ceiling gap, because that repair covers no other flow. The local repair itself splits into two decisions that this fixture cannot express as one, since its gate roadmap_carrier_root_is_minted_scratch ends with roadmap_carrier_path_is_present -- which demands the path ALREADY EXIST as a directory -- and receives the minted path rather than the template, so it is structurally incapable of running before the create. BEFORE creation: admit the TEMPLATE and its location, a predicate over the requested path that needs nothing on disk. AFTER creation: verify the minted directory, its ownership and provenance, and reclaim on any later refusal. These are not equivalent and a control must not promise one outcome for both. Rejecting the template pre-create enforces the PRODUCTION BOUNDARY, because no unauthorized artifact ever comes into existence; reclaiming after the refusal closes only the LEAK, leaving the window between creation and reclamation open to a crash or a failed removal. So a control states its expectation per repair: under a pre-create guard the refusal must arrive with NOTHING created and must not carry the minted-path diagnostic -- a control that asserted 'the directory exists at the moment of refusal' would be asserting today's broken state and calling it green -- while under a cleanup-only repair the directory may exist during the call and must be GONE once it returns, with the claim saying the leak is closed rather than the boundary enforced. Either way the template under test sits under a world-writable disposable parent (/var/tmp, measured 1777), never /var/lib/gunbc: a row must not prove its point by committing the harm it records.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
